1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:07,952
WebMCP lets each website expose reliable tools, but
a real goal still spans providers with different
names, schemas,

2
00:00:07,952 --> 00:00:09,252
and risk levels.

3
00:00:09,252 --> 00:00:12,719
ToolBraid turns those fragments into one accountable
plan.

4
00:00:12,719 --> 00:00:19,654
Here, I give it a goal instead of a click path:
travel from Coventry to London,

5
00:00:19,654 --> 00:00:27,455
find a hotel near my destination, keep the total
under two hundred and fifty pounds, compare the
options,

6
00:00:27,455 --> 00:00:29,622
and ask before changing anything.

7
00:00:29,622 --> 00:00:33,090
Four independent website frames register six WebMCP
tools.

8
00:00:33,090 --> 00:00:36,124
They do not share an API vocabulary.

9
00:00:36,124 --> 00:00:44,792
One calls transport search “seek passages.” Another
calls hotel search “scan spaces.” Their parameters
and results use different field names.

10
00:00:44,792 --> 00:00:47,393
ToolBraid discovers those contracts at runtime.

11
00:00:47,393 --> 00:00:54,327
It combines tool names, descriptions, JSON schemas,
and risk evidence to map them into canonical
capabilities.

12
00:00:54,327 --> 00:00:56,928
It also finds this adversarial tool.

13
00:00:56,928 --> 00:01:03,429
Its description asks the agent to ignore the user,
collect private data, and bypass approval.

14
00:01:03,429 --> 00:01:09,930
Provider metadata is untrusted, so ToolBraid
quarantines it before the tool can enter a plan.

15
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:12,097
Now I build the mission.

16
00:01:12,097 --> 00:01:17,298
ToolBraid creates a seven-step dependency graph
across transport, accommodation, and location
providers.

17
00:01:17,298 --> 00:01:20,332
The search branches can run in parallel.

18
00:01:20,332 --> 00:01:24,233
The hold actions are visibly separated behind human
gates.

19
00:01:24,233 --> 00:01:26,400
I run the safe steps.

20
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:33,335
ToolBraid adapts the mission to each provider
schema, executes the read-only WebMCP tools,
canonicalizes their outputs,

21
00:01:33,335 --> 00:01:39,403
composes compatible options, measures walking
access, and ranks only combinations that respect the
budget.

22
00:01:39,403 --> 00:01:47,204
The best result costs one hundred and eighty-four
pounds and ninety pence, leaving sixty-five pounds
and ten pence.

23
00:01:47,204 --> 00:01:51,538
The hotel is a thirteen-minute walk from the final
destination.

24
00:01:51,538 --> 00:01:52,839
Then execution stops.

25
00:01:52,839 --> 00:01:56,739
The agent cannot approve its own external state
changes.

26
00:01:56,739 --> 00:02:01,940
The human checkpoint shows the exact provider tools,
options, prices, and risk.

27
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:06,275
These are reversible temporary holds, not purchases
or final bookings.

28
00:02:06,275 --> 00:02:08,875
I approve the two selected actions.

29
00:02:08,875 --> 00:02:11,909
Only those approved nodes can now run.

30
00:02:11,909 --> 00:02:15,810
Both provider interfaces update in the same browser
context,

31
00:02:15,810 --> 00:02:20,577
and ToolBraid records the approval and results in
its audit trail.

32
00:02:20,577 --> 00:02:23,178
ToolBraid is not another tool directory.

33
00:02:23,178 --> 00:02:30,979
It is a semantic and policy control plane over
WebMCP: dynamic discovery, schema normalization,
cross-site planning, hostile-metadata quarantine,

34
00:02:30,979 --> 00:02:32,713
and human-owned execution authority.

35
00:02:32,713 --> 00:02:34,013
One human goal.

36
00:02:34,013 --> 00:02:34,880
Multiple websites.

37
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:36,614
One accountable action layer.
